@anon.6e01a69b2c234dfdc5e23dbc943ea0bc.1395 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [1893 x i8] }> <{ [1893 x i8] c"A piece of document content.\0A\0AThis type is at the heart of Typst. All markup you write and most\0A[functions]($function) you call produce content values. You can create a\0Acontent value by enclosing markup in square brackets. This is also how you\0Apass content to functions.\0A\0A# Example\0A```example\0AType of *Hello!* is\0A#type([*Hello!*])\0A```\0A\0AContent can be added with the `+` operator,\0A[joined together]($scripting/#blocks) and multiplied with integers. Wherever\0Acontent is expected, you can also pass a [string]($str) or `{none}`.\0A\0A# Representation\0AContent consists of elements with fields. When constructing an element with\0Aits _element function,_ you provide these fields as arguments and when you\0Ahave a content value, you can access its fields with [field access\0Asyntax]($scripting/#field-access).\0A\0ASome fields are required: These must be provided when constructing an\0Aelement and as a consequence, they are always available through field access\0Aon content of that type. Required fields are marked as such in the\0Adocumentation.\0A\0AMost fields are optional: Like required fields, they can be passed to the\0Aelement function to configure them for a single element. However, these can\0Aalso be configured with [set rules]($styling/#set-rules) to apply them to\0Aall elements within a scope. Optional fields are only available with field\0Aaccess syntax when they were explicitly passed to the element function, not\0Awhen they result from a set rule.\0A\0AEach element has a default appearance. However, you can also completely\0Acustomize its appearance with a [show rule]($styling/#show-rules). The show\0Arule is passed the element. It can access the element's field and produce\0Aarbitrary content from it.\0A\0AIn the web app, you can hover over a content variable to see exactly which\0Aelements the content is composed of and what fields they have.\0AAlternatively, you can inspect the output of the [`repr`] function." }>, align 1
